<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  Gate privacy manifest. docs/06-build-plan.md step 7.1.

	  Gate has no account, no server, no analytics SDK and no crash reporter, so
	  the honest answer to every collection question is "none". Apple defines
	  "collect" as transmitting off device; the DeviceActivityReport extension
	  renders locally inside a sandbox that cannot pass data back to us, and the
	  Screen Time selections are opaque tokens. Nothing leaves the device.

	  NSPrivacyTrackingDomains is deliberately ABSENT. Its presence alongside
	  NSPrivacyTracking=false is invalid and is itself a rejection
	  (ITMS-91054/91055) — an empty array is not the safe default here.

	  The two API categories below are the ones this binary actually reaches.
	  Every target statically links GateKernel, which contains both call sites,
	  so both are declared in both manifests rather than guessed per target:

	    UserDefaults   1C8F.1  the App-Group reason, NOT CA92.1. The suite is
	                           UserDefaults(suiteName: GateID.appGroup), read and
	                           written by the app and by every extension that is
	                           a member of the same App Group
	                           (Kernel/Store/GateStateStore.swift — the state
	                           generation beacon).
	    FileTimestamp  C617.1  metadata of files inside the App Group container.
	                           URL.resourceValues(forKeys: [.contentModificationDateKey])
	                           in Kernel/Store/AppGroupContainer.swift and in
	                           Extensions/ShieldConfiguration (the shield's
	                           staleness stamp).

	  Categories NOT declared, because the code does not hit them — checked, not
	  assumed: SystemBootTime (no mach_absolute_time-family elapsed-time
	  measurement; LockClock deliberately uses absolute dates, and the one
	  mention of ProcessInfo.systemUptime is a comment explaining why it is NOT
	  used), DiskSpace, and ActiveKeyboards. InboxStore orders events by a
	  `createdAt` field decoded from the file's own contents, not by any
	  filesystem timestamp, so it adds no requirement.

	  Do not add a category speculatively. An incorrect declaration is
	  rejectable in its own right.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>1C8F.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>C617.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
